[center][size=4][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Native Equipment Condition (NEC)[/color][/font][/b][/size][/center]

[center]Born as a concept in 2019, it quickly outgrew what the Creation Kit and Papyrus could reach, so I took the standalone DLL route and built it around one idea: weapon and armor condition that looks like it was always in the base game.

Fallout 4 shipped a fairly deep crafting system, with scrap, components and weapon mods, and still left out repair, a missed chance if there ever was one. Wear and repair add another layer to the game economy, giving all that junk and those caps a real use, and they go hand in hand with Survival, where every bullet and every scrap already counts.

In 2026, the game finally got the condition system it should've shipped with, one that looks like part of the game itself and not a mod bolted on as an afterthought. No feature creep, no bloat. [i]It just works™[/i], except this time it actually does.[/center]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Does what, exactly?[/color][/font][/b][/size]

Your weapons and armor [b]wear down as you use them[/b], the way they did in Fallout 3 and New Vegas. Worn weapons and armor lose effectiveness and sell for less. Keep them in shape at a workbench with components or a spare of the same weapon or armor, or pay a trader in caps.

[b]Worn gear can't be modded[/b] until it is repaired to full, so swapping in cheap mods can't cut the repair bill. The [b]Mod lock below[/b] slider in MCM eases this or turns it off.

[b]Done natively[/b], in one F4SE plugin: [b]no ESP and no new assets[/b] besides an optional MCM page and a Papyrus API script for modders. It reads weapons, armor, recipes, perks, leveled lists and game settings from your load order as the game loads, for easier compatibility with other mods, including ones that add new gear.

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Wear and tear[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[b]Weapons[/b]

[list]
[*]Your weapon wears with every shot you fire and every melee hit you land, and harder with a bash or a power attack. Fast automatics like the Minigun wear less per shot, as if they fired at an ordinary automatic's pace.
[*]A worn weapon does less damage, yours and NPCs' alike, down to 66% at 0 condition.
[*]Worn guns lose accuracy and range, and worn automatic weapons fire slower, each down to 75% at 0 condition.
[*]A worn weapon fills the VATS critical meter slower, down to 50% of its rate at 0 condition, like in Fallout 3, where worn weapons landed fewer critical hits.
[*]Your guns can jam once they drop below 50% condition. [b]Jams below[/b] and [b]Jam chance at 0 condition[/b] in MCM set where they start and how often.
[*]Your weapon is [b]unequipped automatically[/b] when it breaks, and can't be equipped again until it is repaired.
[*]NPC weapons wear too, by default a third as fast as yours. A weapon worn out to 0 stays in their hands.
[/list]

[b]Armor and clothing[/b]

[list]
[*]Every hit that lands wears the armor and clothing on the limb it struck, yours and NPCs' alike. Explosions wear every piece, and pieces that cover no limb, like a mask or a super mutant's armor, share each hit between them.
[*]Worn armor protects less, yours and NPCs' alike, down to 66% at 0 condition.
[*]Armor mods that change a piece's Health, by a multiplier or a flat amount, make it wear slower or faster by the same share.
[*]A [b]broken piece[/b] stays on and keeps its bonuses, like a +1 to a stat or a legendary effect, until you take it off. After that it can't be equipped again until it is repaired.
[*]Apparel with no armor rating or bonus, like hats and rings, wears like armor. With [b]Plain apparel never wears[/b] switched on in MCM, it never wears and shows no CND.
[*][b]Power armor[/b] wears and is repaired at the power armor station as it always was, but a damaged piece now sells for less, and so does a drained fusion core.
[/list]

[b]All gear[/b]

[list]
[*]Price drops faster than condition, on the Fallout 3 and New Vegas curve: an item at half condition is worth 35% of its full price, whether you buy it or sell it.
[*]Worn gear [b]scraps into fewer components[/b] at a workbench and in workshop mode, down to 1 component for a broken item, its most common one.
[*]The weapons and armor that companions and other essential NPCs come with never wear on them, since some of it can never be handed to you to repair. Once an item has passed through your hands, it wears like any other item you own, on you or on them.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Loot with a history[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Weapons and armor turn up [b]already worn[/b]. Each gets its condition the first time it lands in an inventory: an NPC's, a container's, a trader's stock, or yours when you pick up one that was lying around. That happens only once, so its condition is never rerolled, no matter who carries it or where it gets dropped.
[*]The condition is a roll, weighted by where the item came from and who carried it. A raider's pipe gun has seen better days, while a well equipped faction like the Brotherhood or the Gunners keeps its gear in better shape. Gear lying around lands at around 45 to 70%, while [b]most quest rewards arrive new[/b], and so does everything companions and other essential NPCs start with.
[*]Legendary gear you find in chests or buy from traders arrives [b]new[/b]. Legendaries dropped by enemies still turn up worn.
[*]About 1 item in 100 ignores all that: a raider can drop a pristine rifle, and a Courser a beaten one.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Repair at the workbench[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]At the weapon or armor workbench, select a worn item and a REPAIR button takes RENAME's place until it is back at full. Press it, choose how far to repair it in steps of 10%, and pay in the components it is built from. Beside each step the box shows what it adds to the item's damage or resistances by type, and the box of spares does the same for each spare, in columns unless [b]Repair rows in columns[/b] is off. Gear with no scrap recipe, like Grognak's Axe, costs what gear of its kind most often scraps into, and wears at the same rate. Short of components, you get the power armor station's list of what the repair takes, with the ones you lack faded.
[*]Carrying a spare of the same item? [b]USE A SPARE[/b] in the repair box takes it apart for the repair: it restores as much condition as its scrap is worth, and the components left over go to your inventory. [b]Spare worth[/b] sets how much condition a spare restores and Scrapper raises it, but you never get back more components than it holds.
[*]Barely worn gear, above 95%, gets a MEND button instead, which fixes it on the spot [b]for free[/b]. The 95% is the [b]Free mend above[/b] setting: 100 makes every repair cost components, and 0 mends everything but broken gear for free.
[*][b]Crafting perks[/b] like Gun Nut, Science!, Blacksmith and Armorer make repairs take fewer components, down to half at the top rank. The perk behind most of an item's components sets the discount, and each rank's description says how much. The perks count too when a skill mod like Classic NV Skill System grants them from your skills.
[*]A repair [b]earns the same XP[/b] as crafting a mod from those components.
[*]With [b]Workbench repair cost[/b] at 0, every repair is free and done on the spot, broken gear included.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Repair at a trader[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Out of spare components? Gun shops, armorers and clothiers repair their kind of gear [b]for caps[/b], and those caps stay in the trader's till, ready to buy the loot you haul in next. On the barter screen, select a worn item on your side and a REPAIR button appears if they fix that kind of gear. Click it, or press C on the keyboard or the left bumper on a pad. Each step shows its price and what it adds to the item's damage or resistances.
[*]What a trader repairs, and how far, depends on what they restock. Plenty of guns on the shelves, or a few beside plenty of ammunition, grenades and mines, means they fix guns, and the more kinds of gun they stock, the further they repair them. Armor and clothing work the same way, and so do traders from other mods. A new settlement weapon stand stops at 30%, Lucas Miller's armor caravan at 70% and Ronnie Shaw's fully upgraded store at 90%, give or take with your level. [b]Only the best stocked shops repair to 100%[/b], and finding them is up to you.
[*]Traders also sell each kind of gear in better shape the further they repair that kind. Where they repair a kind to 30% or not at all, they sell it at 30% to 50% condition, and the best shops at 85% to 100%. So a gun shop that repairs guns to 90% sells its guns in good shape, but the odd piece of armor on its shelves comes in much worse shape, since it doesn't repair armor. They sell apparel with no armor rating or bonus at 100%.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]See it at a glance[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]CND on the item cards, with the worn damage, resistances, fire rate, accuracy and range.
[*]CND bars on the HUD ammo counter, the power armor dash, the pickup prompt and the Pip-Boy apparel figure, and condition meters on the quick container rows and a food's freshness. Each HUD bar has its own switch, place and size in MCM.
[*]Broken items fade out in the Pip-Boy lists.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Make it yours[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]With MCM, every setting changes on the spot. The main page shows NEC's version, Log and any part another mod took over. The Weapons, Armor, Loot, Repairs, UI and Little extras pages hold the rest: switch features off, or tune wear, penalties, loot condition, prices and repair costs.
[*]Without MCM, [b]NEC.ini[/b] in Data\F4SE\Plugins explains every setting. Copy a line into [b]NEC_custom.ini[/b] beside it, under the same section heading, change the value and restart the game.
[*]Either way your changes go in NEC_custom.ini, and NEC.ini stays as it ships. The mod comes without a NEC_custom.ini, so an update never wipes your settings. With Mod Organizer 2, keep NEC_custom.ini in its own mod or in overwrite.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Little extras[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Food spoiling[/b], off until you switch it on: food you carry and food on the dead spoils over game days, slower in coolers and fridges, as in Fallout 76. Spoiled food is its own row at 0 condition, heals less and gives more rads. Cooking stations won't use it, and a chemistry station turns it into Fertilizer.
[spoiler]
[list]
[*]Food you or a companion carry, and food on the dead, spoils. Coolers and unbroken fridges slow it down and mark their food with a frost icon.
[*]The card of a spoiled food shows its own healing and rads. Eating it heals only 10% as much and gives more rads, which Lead Belly cuts as it cuts the food's own. In Survival it heals nothing and fills hunger or thirst only a tenth as much as the fresh food would. Survival has a minimum that every meal fills, and spoiled food still gets that minimum.
[*]In Survival, spoiled food is 5 times as likely to make you sick as the fresh food. Food that never makes you sick, such as cooked food, takes the smallest chance any food in your load order has.
[*]With worn loot on, food you find is already partly spoiled, while food you buy, cook, harvest or get as a reward starts fresh.
[*]MCM's Little extras page turns food spoiling on and sets how long raw and cooked food stay fresh, how much coolers and fridges slow it down, how much spoiled food makes 1 Fertilizer, its healing outside Survival, its hunger in Survival, its extra rads, its sickness in Survival and whether the fridge icon shows.
[*]NEC.food.log, beside NEC.log, shows the food settings in force, what each food and fridge file does, and every food with the kind NEC gave it.
[/list]
[/spoiler]
[*]7 loading screen tips on wear, repairs, broken gear, jams, loot and food spoiling, in the vanilla style and as likely to show as any ordinary loading screen. A tip about a feature you switch off never shows.
[*]Vanilla fix: the inspect screen at a trader shows the right price for the side the item is on.
[*]Vanilla fix: a workbench confirmation box with a long list of components shows more rows and scrolls with the mouse wheel, the arrow keys, the D-pad and W and S.
[*]A console command for testing: [font=Courier New]srm[/font] brings the weapon in hand back to full, and [font=Courier New]srm 25[/font] sets it to 25%, any number from 0 to 100. Add armor, as in [font=Courier New]srm armor[/font] or [font=Courier New]srm 25 armor[/font], for every piece you have on that wears.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Compatibility[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Runs on Fallout 4 1.11.240 (AE) and 1.10.163 (OG).
[*]No ESP, so it takes no plugin slot.
[*]Works with UI replacers like FallUI and DEF_UI. The Weapon CND bar finds a free spot by the ammo counter on any HUD layout and stays on screen at any resolution. A part NEC can't find is skipped and named in NEC.log or the bug report logs, while wear, damage and prices work as ever.
[*]NEC works alongside other DLL mods that change the same things. When one takes over one of NEC's features, NEC lets it, and NEC's main MCM page and NEC.log name that mod and what is off.
[*]The plugin's own text comes in every language the game ships in. The rest is vanilla text. Every language but English was translated by AI, not by a person, so some lines may read a little off. To fix a line or add a language, edit or copy a file in Data\F4SE\Plugins\NEC\Translations, as en.ini there explains. For Simplified Chinese, copy zhhans.ini over cn.ini.
[*]Item stats, recipes, leveled lists and NPC outfits that a DLL mod like RobCo Patcher changes as the game starts count in wear, repair costs and loot condition, since NEC reads them again at every save load and new game.
[*]Both vanilla fixes under Little extras can be switched off, for a load order where another mod already fixes them.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Exclusion list[/color][/font][/b][/size]

To have NEC leave some weapons or armor alone, such as gear with a condition of its own, list them in a file in Data\F4SE\Plugins\NEC\Exclude, 1 item a line: the plugin that adds the item, a bar, and the item's form ID from FO4Edit. The plugin name already says where the item comes from, so the load order digits can be left out, and 0x and leading zeros are optional.

[spoiler][code]; MyExclusions.ini
Gas Masks of the Wasteland.esp|002672    ; M17 Gas Mask, no load order
Gas Masks of the Wasteland.esp|35A7      ; PMK-1 Gas Mask, no leading zeros
Gas Masks of the Wasteland.esp|0x000F99  ; GP-5 Gas Mask, with 0x
Gas Masks of the Wasteland.esp|0100928E  ; PPM-88 Gas Mask, whole FO4Edit ID
ccbgsfo4116-heavyflamer.esl|809          ; Heavy Incinerator, light plugin
ccbgsfo4116-heavyflamer.esl|FE000809     ; the same, whole FO4Edit ID[/code]

For a light plugin (.esl or ESL flagged), write the last 3 digits or the whole ID, since those are the only digits that count.

NEC.log lists what each file leaves alone and any line it skips. Repair broken gear before you list it, since the game won't let you put on an item at 0 condition. NEC ships Exclude\NEC_Example.ini, an example that changes nothing as it is.[/spoiler]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Food and fridge lists[/color][/font][/b][/size]

Food spoiling sorts every food in your load order into 4 kinds from the game data: raw, crop, cooked and never spoils. For a mod whose food it sorts wrong, copy the food's line from NEC.food.log into a file in Data\F4SE\Plugins\NEC\Food and change its kind. List a mod's fridges and coolers in Data\F4SE\Plugins\NEC\Fridge by the container's base form, as FO4Edit shows it. Both take the exclusion list's line form.

[spoiler]NEC sorts a food by the first rule that fits. A drink never spoils. Food a cooking station recipe makes is cooked. Food harvested from a plant, or with the FruitOrVegetable keyword, is a crop. Food with one of Survival's disease risk keywords is raw. Everything else, such as canned and boxed food, never spoils. A line you add overrides these rules.

[code]; Food\MyFood.ini
MyFoodMod.esp|000801 = raw
MyFoodMod.esp|000802 = never spoils

; Fridge\MyKitchen.ini
MyKitchenMod.esp|000803    ; MyKitchen_Freezer01[/code]

NEC.food.log lists what each file sets and any line it skips. NEC ships Fridge\NEC_Default.ini with the vanilla coolers and unbroken fridges. Food\NEC_Example.ini and Fridge\NEC_Example.ini are examples that change nothing as they are.[/spoiler]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Trader list[/color][/font][/b][/size]

To set by hand how far a trader repairs a kind of gear, add a line to a file in Data\F4SE\Plugins\NEC\Traders.

[spoiler][code]; Traders\MyTraders.ini
Fallout4.esm|002CB9 = armor none    ; Arturo Rodriguez repairs no armor
Fallout4.esm|1679CE = armor 90      ; Lucas Miller's caravan, armor to 90%
Fallout4.esm|10C03F = weapons 50    ; every settlement weapon store, to 50%[/code]

A line names a trader, a kind of gear (weapons, armor or clothing) and how far they repair it, from 30 to 100 in steps of 10. [font=Courier New]none[/font] means they don't repair that kind. [font=Courier New]measured[/font] leaves it to NEC, as if there were no line, which exempts 1 trader from their faction's line.

The ID can be the trader's ref ID, their base ID or the form ID of the faction they trade under, which covers a whole caravan or every store of a kind. The ref ID beats the base ID, which beats the faction. The fastest way to get a ref ID is to click the trader in the console. To get all 3, turn on [b]Bug report logs[/b] and trade with them. NEC.ui.trace.log then lists them in this form, the ref ID as placed and the base ID as NPC. A ref ID that starts with FF belongs to an NPC the game created during play, such as a spawned settler. NEC skips such a line, because that ID is stored in the save (in another save, or after that NPC is removed, it can point to a different NPC). Use their base ID or faction instead.

NEC.log lists what each file sets and any line it skips. NEC ships Traders\NEC_Example.ini, an example that changes nothing as it is.[/spoiler]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Requirements[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Fallout 4 1.11.240 (AE) or 1.10.163 (OG)
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/42147]Fallout 4 Script Extender (F4SE)[/url], version 0.7.9 on AE or 0.6.23 on OG
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/47327]Address Library for F4SE Plugins[/url]
[*]Optional: [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/21497]Mod Configuration Menu[/url], version 1.39 on OG
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Installation[/color][/font][/b][/size]

Install with your mod manager, or copy the F4SE, MCM and Scripts folders into Fallout 4's Data folder. Launch the game through F4SE.

NEC.pdb, beside NEC.dll, is only there for crash logs. The game itself never uses it. If the game ever crashes, a crash logger like [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/84214]Addictol[/url] can read it to point at the exact line of NEC's code involved, which makes a bug report much easier to fix. You can delete it without changing anything in play, but then a crash log can only say that NEC was involved, not where.

Installing it to a playthrough in progress? What you carry and store stays at 100%. NEC rolls an item when the game first spawns it, so loot spawned before you install or update NEC, or while Worn loot (bSpawnCondition) is off, comes out at 100% the first time it changes hands. Places you haven't visited yet, new loot and trader restocks roll as usual.

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Uninstalling[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[b]Repair anything broken first[/b], at a workbench, at a trader or, for gear you still have on, with [font=Courier New]srm[/font] in the console. Fallout 4 still has the rule from Fallout 3 and New Vegas that an item at 0 condition can't be equipped, and once NEC is gone nothing in the game can repair it, so it stays unusable.

After that, [b]removing the mod is safe.[/b] It has no ESP and keeps no save data of its own. NEC keeps each item's condition in its health, one of the small tags, called extra data, that Fallout 4 already saves on an item, like its custom name. Power armor pieces use health too. That health is all that stays in your save. On anything but power armor the game ignores it for damage, protection and price, so what is left behind does nothing there, except that identical items at different conditions stay in separate stacks in your inventory.

A save made without any mod calling NEC's Papyrus API holds no trace of it. Once a mod that uses NEC's Papyrus API has run in your save, keep NEC installed for that playthrough: without it that mod's calls fail on every load, and taking that mod's plugin, its ESP, ESM or ESL, out of a running playthrough is never safe.

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Under the hood[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[spoiler]

[b]Where condition lives[/b]

[list]
[*]NEC keeps condition in ExtraHealth on the item's inventory stack. Power armor keeps its health there too, and a fusion core its charge.
[*]ExtraHealth 1.0 means new and 0 means broken. An item with no ExtraHealth counts as new.
[*]Full is stored as 0.9999999, because setting ExtraHealth to 1.0 deletes it.
[*]NEC stores the game day food started to spoil in ExtraTimeLeft on its inventory stack, which the game itself only uses for lights, and gives spoiled food an ExtraHealth just above 0, since the game won't let you use an item at 0.
[*]Papyrus reads condition with ObjectReference.GetItemHealthPercent. It only works on an item lying in the world, not in an inventory.
[*]GetItemHealthPercent returns -1 for an item with no ExtraHealth. While Worn loot is on, it also returns -1 for loose loot that hasn't rolled its condition yet.
[/list]

[b]Leftovers from Fallout 3 and Skyrim[/b]

[list]
[*]Fallout 3's check that blocks equipping an item at 0 ExtraHealth is still in the game, message and all. In vanilla you only meet it with a broken power armor piece, since nothing else has health.
[*]The same check would also keep a broken item from coming off, or from going back on after the barber chair. NEC lets both through.
[*]sWeaponBreak is a broken weapon message the game never shows. NEC shows it when your weapon breaks.
[*][font=Courier New]srm[/font] is Fallout 3's ShowRepairMenu console command. In vanilla it does nothing.
[*]The Item Condition on a leveled list or container entry (COED) still sets ExtraHealth, but only on armor or ammo with a Health value in its record. In vanilla that means power armor and fusion cores. NEC leaves it as it is.
[*]The vanilla damage, armor rating and price formulas already read ExtraHealth. They treat it as Skyrim's tempering bonus, so ExtraHealth below 1.0 changes none of them.
[*]NEC scales each result where the game works it out. That is also why damaged power armor and drained fusion cores sell for less.
[/list]

[b]Read from the load order[/b]

[i]What wears[/i]

[list]
[*]New weapons and weapon mods need no patch for wear. A weapon's wear per use depends on:
[list]
[*]its damage, against the median damage of every weapon in the load order that wears
[*]the average component value of its recipes
[*]for a fast automatic, the median fire rate of the load order's automatics
[/list]
[*]A hit wears the armor on the body part its hit event names. The wearer's race maps each body part to biped slots.
[*]A hit with no body part, like a blast, wears every piece on a slot some body part maps to.
[*]Pieces on slots that no body part maps to share each hit between them. A wearable among them that gives no protection or bonus takes the same share, but doesn't count in the split. With [b]Plain apparel never wears[/b] on, such a wearable never wears.
[*]NEC leaves out grenades, mines, unplayable weapons and power armor.
[*]It also leaves out wearables with no name, and race or NPC skins (WNAM), when they give no protection or bonus and fit no armor mod that adds one.
[*]Which armor mods add protection, an effect or a bonus, and which pieces are skins, is read as the game loads and again at every save load and new game.
[*]For an armor mod that adds a flat amount of Health, a piece with no Health of its own, which in vanilla is every piece but power armor, counts as the median Health of the load order's armor that has one. A piece with no Health mod wears as ever, and no mod makes a piece wear more than 10 times as fast.
[/list]

[i]Essential NPCs[/i]

[list]
[*]On an essential actor, items from its own outfit (ExtraOutfitItem) never wear. Neither do items from its record's inventory, on a plain line or a leveled one (ExtraLeveledItem).
[*]An item with an owner (ExtraOwnership) still wears on an essential actor. Anything you give a companion through its trade menu has one, naming you or whoever you stole it from.
[/list]

[i]Loot[/i]

[list]
[*]Loot rolls its condition once, when the game first spawns the item. Loading a save never rolls anything.
[*]When the game spawns a loose item in the world, NEC marks it as not rolled yet with an ExtraHealth of 0.99999994. Whoever first picks it up rolls its condition.
[*]With the [b]Pickup CND bar[/b] on, a loose item gets its condition the first time you look at it, the same roll the pickup would make, and the save keeps it. A loose item with a faction owner (ExtraOwnership) rolls on the barter screen as the trader's stock. Vanilla places none. NEC reads the item from the pickup prompt as the game fills it (HUDRolloverModel::Update), so it is the same item E picks up.
[*]A loose item with no ExtraHealth at all spawned before you installed or updated NEC, or while Worn loot was off. It comes out at 100%.
[*]The roll uses the leveled list the item came from (ExtraLeveledItem), ranked against every other list of its kind. It also uses the armor the item's owner was given.
[*]Items of an NPC whose base record is essential start at 100%.
[*]The barter screen lists the trader's stock, plus loose items nearby that the trader's faction owns. NEC rolls such an item once, when the barter screen first lists it. The one you buy keeps that roll.
[*]The game saves the random loot it puts in a container. A container of set items is rebuilt from its record whenever the save holds nothing for it.
[*]So once NEC rolls a container of set items, it records the container as changed, the way the game does for random loot. The save then keeps its conditions.
[*]An item with no ExtraHealth starts at 100% when it reaches you through a Papyrus AddItem or RemoveItem, or the console.
[*]AddItem and RemoveItem also reset an item to 100% when it is a quest item (in a Quest Object alias) from anyone but you.
[*]They do the same for an item from a living character who isn't your companion, like the gun Paladin Brandis gives you. Otherwise, an item from a corpse keeps its roll.
[/list]

[i]Repairs and traders[/i]

[list]
[*]NEC finds which crafting perk discounts a repair in the HasPerk conditions on the item's recipes.
[*]What a trader repairs comes from the leveled lists their vendor chests restock from, because no item carries the weapon, armor and clothing VendorItem keywords.
[*]Without those keywords, NEC counts a piece as clothing if it sits on biped slot 33, the body slot, or protects against nothing.
[*]A wearable that gives no protection or bonus counts toward no kind, since traders sell it new.
[/list]

[i]Read again at every save load[/i]

[list]
[*]At every save load and new game, NEC reads these again: what each recipe builds, what each crafting perk unlocks, what each leveled list hands out and the armor each NPC is given. It reads them after any DLL mod that patches them as the game loads.
[*]What a script adds to a leveled list at run time is left out: NEC reads the lists as the plugins ship them.
[/list]

[b]Settings and other mods[/b]

[list]
[*]NEC's MCM page and its sub pages are only a config.json. NEC answers MCM's GetModSetting, SetModSetting and GetFullName calls from its own settings and translations.
[*]While one of NEC's pages is open, NEC switches MCM's settings list from Multi-Line text mode to None, so each note is only as tall as its words. It switches back once another mod's page opens.
[*]NEC patches call sites and function table slots at F4SE's PostPostLoad, with no detours.
[*]It reads them back once every file has loaded, at each save load and new game, and whenever the main or pause menu opens.
[*]If another DLL hooked a place first, NEC's hook calls it. If NEC can't call it safely, NEC's change at that place stays off.
[*]If a DLL hooks a place after NEC and calls NEC's hook, both run. If it doesn't, NEC's change at that place stays off while that DLL is installed.
[*]The barter screen, a workbench's item list and its confirmation box are an exception. There NEC waits until the screen has opened once. If the call reaches NEC's hook by then, NEC keeps its change.
[*]A feature that can't work without a place switches off with it.
[/list]
[/spoiler]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Found a bug?[/color][/font][/b][/size]

If a feature seems to do nothing, another DLL mod may have taken it over. Load a save, then look at NEC's main page in MCM. It names that mod.

For anything else, share NEC's logs:
[list=1]
[*]Turn on the full logs:
[list]
[*]With MCM: on NEC's main page, under Log, set [b]Log detail[/b] to debug and switch [b]Bug report logs[/b] on.
[*]Without MCM: add these lines to NEC_custom.ini in Data\F4SE\Plugins, then restart the game:
[code][Log]
sLogLevel=debug
bTraceLogs=true[/code]
[/list]
[*]Play until the bug happens again.
[*]Open the folder Documents\My Games\Fallout4\F4SE\NEC and share these files:
[list]
[*]NEC.log, NEC.trace.log, NEC.ui.trace.log, NEC.npc.trace.log and NEC.food.trace.log, and NEC.food.log with food spoiling on.
[*]NEC.papyrus.log and NEC.papyrus.trace.log, for a problem with a mod that uses NEC's Papyrus API.
[*]If the game crashed and you started it again, also the files with a 1 in the name, like NEC.1.log. They are from the game that crashed. NEC keeps your last 10 games this way, and a higher number is older.
[*]The crash log, if the game crashed, like crash-2026-10-01-21-15-31.log from [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/84214]Addictol[/url], 1 folder up in Documents\My Games\Fallout4\F4SE
[/list]
[*]Say what you did right before it went wrong, and post it all on the Bugs tab here or [url=https://github.com/hxef/NativeEquipmentCondition/issues]as an issue on GitHub[/url].
[*]Switch the full logs off again. They get big fast.
[/list]

[size=3][b][font=Georgia][color=#1AFF80]Papyrus API[/color][/font][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]NEC ships a Papyrus script that your mod can call to read and change the condition of any weapon, armor or food, read the repair costs and the curves, and hear when condition changes, an item breaks, a gun jams or food spoils.
[*]Every function is global, so a call reads NEC.GetCondition(akItem), and it works the same on OG and AE.
[*]The [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/articles/6727]Papyrus API[/url] article lists every function and event with some examples. The same text ships as NEC_Papyrus_API.txt in Data\F4SE\Plugins.
[*]Compile against NEC.psc in Data\Scripts\Source\User, and never ship NEC.pex or NEC.psc with your mod.
[/list]

